PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The panel maintains a unanimous consensus on Choice B (Pleuroperitoneal membrane defect). The clinical presentation of respiratory distress, severe macrosomia, micrognathia, and a left hemithoracic fluid-filled density in a newborn prior to aerophagia represents congenital diaphragmatic hernia (Bochdalek hernia). The primary embryological defect is failure of the pleuroperitoneal membrane to close the pericardioperitoneal canal, with secondary pulmonary hypoplasia resulting from compression. All other answer choices represent secondary effects, extrinsic maternal conditions, or unrelated developmental anomalies.
}

